artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a hierarchy of temporal contact networks indexed by population scales s = 1, 2, ..., S (for instance, household, workplace/school, city, country, global). At each scale s, let G_s(t) = (V_s, E_s(t)) be a time-varying graph on a vertex set V_s, with the edge set E_s(t) evolving according to a prescribed stochastic or deterministic rule with characteristic time scale τ_s. Couple these scales by specifying inclusion/aggregation maps π_{s+1,s}: V_s → V_{s+1} (so that nodes at a finer scale are grouped into nodes at a coarser scale) together with cross-scale interaction rules that allow contacts realized at one scale to influence the edge dynamics and the spread of infection at other scales. On this coupled multi-scale temporal network, run a compartmental epidemic process (for example SIS or SIR) with per-contact transmission rate β and recovery rate γ.

Develop a mathematical framework that:
(a) defines a meaningful coupled multi-scale temporal contact network model in which the temporal dynamics at every scale are non-trivial and interact;
(b) characterizes the epidemic threshold (the critical value of β/γ above which a macroscopic outbreak occurs) as a function of the temporal statistics and topologies of the networks at each scale and of the cross-scale coupling;
(c) identifies the qualitatively new epidemic and network-structural phenomena (e.g., metastability, scale-dependent thresholds, emergent bursts, localization, or synchronization of activity) that arise from coupling temporal networks across scales, and that are absent when each scale is treated as a closed, isolated population.

In particular, determine under what conditions the multi-scale epidemic threshold is determined by a single dominant scale versus by a genuinely multi-scale combination, and quantify the corrections to single-scale threshold formulas induced by the coupling.

